WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.234
Welcome to Building Defender XDR,

2
00:00:02.234 --> 00:00:06.212
a developer workbook in the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

3
00:00:06.212 --> 00:00:10.013
This is Chapter 2: onboarding
coverage and sensor health.

4
00:00:10.013 --> 00:00:14.857
A policy assigned to a group says nothing
about the devices that never checked in,

5
00:00:14.857 --> 00:00:18.762
so coverage is counted against the
inventory, one device at a time.

6
00:00:18.762 --> 00:00:22.882
We'll start from the guide's coverage
query and Learn's device checks,

7
00:00:22.882 --> 00:00:27.664
read how the kit joins each device to
the inventory and the exception register,

8
00:00:27.664 --> 00:00:31.637
meet the device collector the next
chapters use, and walk two labs.

9
00:00:31.637 --> 00:00:34.270
The chapter is a draft awaiting review;

10
00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:38.080
the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

11
00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:43.005
Why count this way?

12
00:00:43.005 --> 00:00:47.593
A policy assigned to a group says nothing
about the devices that never checked in.

13
00:00:47.593 --> 00:00:50.116
If you count against Defender's own list,

14
00:00:50.116 --> 00:00:53.329
a device that never onboarded
is simply invisible.

15
00:00:53.329 --> 00:00:55.692
So the denominator is the inventory.

16
00:00:55.692 --> 00:01:00.139
By the end of this chapter you can produce
the weekly coverage file the guide's

17
00:01:00.139 --> 00:01:01.806
evidence checklist asks for,

18
00:01:01.806 --> 00:01:05.767
with a ticket for every device that
isn't protected and isn't excepted.

19
00:01:05.767 --> 00:01:10.286
It's for the developers who maintain the
kit, the operator who owns onboarding,

20
00:01:10.286 --> 00:01:14.960
and the security officer who reconciles
the system component inventory.

21
00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:20.022
Here's what's already there.

22
00:01:20.022 --> 00:01:22.940
On a device, Learn names
the sense service and

23
00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:26.173
the registry value onboarding
state, equal to one.

24
00:01:26.173 --> 00:01:30.931
Across the fleet, the guide's query takes
the latest DeviceInfo row per Windows

25
00:01:30.931 --> 00:01:35.103
device and counts them by onboarding
status and sensor health state,

26
00:01:35.103 --> 00:01:36.786
run through Graph and filed.

27
00:01:36.786 --> 00:01:42.372
Learn's device inventory reference lists
the sensor health states: active; inactive,

28
00:01:42.372 --> 00:01:46.044
which typically means no
contact for seven days or more;

29
00:01:46.044 --> 00:01:50.035
no sensor data; impaired
communications; and misconfigured.

30
00:01:50.035 --> 00:01:54.460
Intune's onboarding report shows
the same from the Intune side.

31
00:01:58.210 --> 00:01:59.685
What does a count leave to you?

32
00:01:59.685 --> 00:02:03.335
It can't name the device that
needs a ticket, so the kit runs the

33
00:02:03.335 --> 00:02:07.422
same query one row per device, and
keeps the guide's summary beside it.

34
00:02:07.422 --> 00:02:09.879
Defender only lists devices it knows,

35
00:02:09.879 --> 00:02:14.872
so every inventory device is classified,
whether Defender reports it or not.

36
00:02:14.872 --> 00:02:18.630
A lab device with no path to the
service looks like a gap forever,

37
00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:23.184
so the exception register makes it
excepted, and a lapsed exception a ticket.

38
00:02:23.184 --> 00:02:27.210
Names differ, so they're
compared without the DNS suffix.

39
00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:31.440
And Learn doesn't list the exact
strings advanced hunting returns,

40
00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:36.640
so only listed values count as healthy;
anything else is printed and flagged.

41
00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:44.802
The coverage check gives every
in-scope device one of seven statuses.

42
00:02:44.802 --> 00:02:47.691
Protected: onboarded
with an active sensor.

43
00:02:47.691 --> 00:02:51.927
Not active: onboarded, but the
sensor is in any other state.

44
00:02:51.927 --> 00:02:57.003
Not onboarded: not onboarded, or not
reported at all, with no exception.

45
00:02:57.003 --> 00:03:01.915
Excepted: a valid exception, with an
approver and an expiry still ahead.

46
00:03:01.915 --> 00:03:03.377
Exception expired.

47
00:03:03.377 --> 00:03:07.528
Not in inventory: Defender
reports it, the inventory doesn't.

48
00:03:07.528 --> 00:03:08.615
And out of scope.

49
00:03:08.615 --> 00:03:10.090
Four of those are tickets.

50
00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:13.987
The run writes the coverage file,
the tickets, a by-ring summary,

51
00:03:13.987 --> 00:03:18.877
the protected-coverage share, which is
the guide's first metric, and a manifest.

52
00:03:18.877 --> 00:03:22.810
A missing column fails the run
rather than reading as empty.

53
00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:29.172
The chapter also ships a device collector.

54
00:03:29.172 --> 00:03:34.024
It runs on one Windows device, elevated,
reads only, and writes one JSON file:

55
00:03:34.024 --> 00:03:39.184
the sense service and the onboarding state
and sensor version from the registry;

56
00:03:39.184 --> 00:03:44.035
tamper and real-time protection and
the antivirus mode; network protection,

57
00:03:44.035 --> 00:03:48.271
controlled folder access and the
attack surface reduction rules;

58
00:03:48.271 --> 00:03:50.735
and the firewall profiles as enforced.

59
00:03:50.735 --> 00:03:55.347
Chapters 3, 4 and 7 compare
these files with the plans.

60
00:03:55.347 --> 00:03:59.750
For servers under Azure Arc that lack
the Defender extension, the Microsoft

61
00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:01.707
automation kit already has a task,

62
00:04:01.707 --> 00:04:04.920
so this chapter links it
instead of rebuilding it.

63
00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:09.720
Who's involved?

64
00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:13.116
The operator owns onboarding:
every device that isn't

65
00:04:13.116 --> 00:04:16.358
active for a week is a ticket,
worked within the week.

66
00:04:16.358 --> 00:04:19.865
The security officer reconciles
coverage against the system

67
00:04:19.865 --> 00:04:23.507
component inventory and keeps the
weekly file for twelve months.

68
00:04:23.507 --> 00:04:27.369
And the platform owner reviews the
exception register each quarter.

69
00:04:27.369 --> 00:04:30.294
One caution: the onboarding
and sensor strings in

70
00:04:30.294 --> 00:04:33.432
the kit's configuration are
the ones the portal shows.

71
00:04:33.432 --> 00:04:37.910
Check them against your test tenant's
raw output before the first live run.

72
00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:43.760
Lab 1, in the kit's folder.

73
00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:46.097
Step 1 runs the coverage check.

74
00:04:46.097 --> 00:04:51.209
Nine of fourteen in-scope devices are
protected: sixty-four point three percent.

75
00:04:51.209 --> 00:04:52.660
Ring 0 is whole.

76
00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:57.091
Five tickets: a sensor with impaired
communications, an inactive sensor,

77
00:04:57.091 --> 00:05:00.414
a device Defender can see
but that was never onboarded,

78
00:05:00.414 --> 00:05:03.737
a server whose exception
expired at the end of October,

79
00:05:03.737 --> 00:05:07.873
and a device Defender reports that
the inventory doesn't list at all.

80
00:05:07.873 --> 00:05:13.730
The kiosk in the isolated lab isn't a
ticket: its exception runs into next year.

81
00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:20.721
Step 2 reads the tickets
as the operator gets them,

82
00:05:20.721 --> 00:05:25.068
each with its note: the expired
exception names its reason and date.

83
00:05:25.068 --> 00:05:29.706
Step 3 reads the guide's own query
for the same estate, as counts.

84
00:05:29.706 --> 00:05:31.950
It can't say which device is inactive,

85
00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:34.793
or that two in-scope
devices aren't there at all.

86
00:05:34.793 --> 00:05:38.855
Step 4 reads one device's state,
as the collector writes it.

87
00:05:38.855 --> 00:05:41.355
Look at tamper protection: false.

88
00:05:41.355 --> 00:05:42.893
Chapter 3 finds it.

89
00:05:42.893 --> 00:05:46.890
The last step runs the chapter's
tests, and they all pass.

90
00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:55.198
Lab 2 uses a test tenant, never
production, with at least one Windows test

91
00:05:55.198 --> 00:06:00.065
device onboarded through Intune's
endpoint detection and response policy.

92
00:06:00.065 --> 00:06:02.496
Sign in with the hunting
read scope and run

93
00:06:02.496 --> 00:06:05.065
the coverage check
without the fixture path.

94
00:06:05.065 --> 00:06:08.360
Then open the raw device rows
and compare the onboarding

95
00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:11.165
and sensor strings with
the kit's configuration.

96
00:06:11.165 --> 00:06:16.115
A row flagged unrecognized means they
differ; record it before changing anything.

97
00:06:16.115 --> 00:06:18.827
On the test device, run
the collector elevated:

98
00:06:18.827 --> 00:06:22.753
it should show the sense service
running and onboarding state one.

99
00:06:22.753 --> 00:06:25.450
Nothing changed, so you just sign out.

100
00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:32.738
Here's what the coverage check
proves, against NIST's catalog.

101
00:06:32.738 --> 00:06:36.776
Every in-scope device onboarded
with an active sensor, excepted,

102
00:06:36.776 --> 00:06:40.662
or a ticket, supports the system
component inventory control.

103
00:06:40.662 --> 00:06:44.762
The sensor reporting, ring by
ring, supports system monitoring.

104
00:06:44.762 --> 00:06:47.936
And a device that Defender
sees but the inventory

105
00:06:47.936 --> 00:06:51.263
doesn't list is an inventory
finding in its own right.

106
00:06:51.263 --> 00:06:55.451
The indicators are FedRAMP's, as
the implementation guide maps them.

107
00:06:55.451 --> 00:06:59.620
The records support these controls;
the assessor decides them.

108
00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:04.770
What breaks in production?

109
00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:08.326
Counting against Defender's
own list hides every device

110
00:07:08.326 --> 00:07:11.807
that never onboarded; the
inventory is the denominator.

111
00:07:11.807 --> 00:07:17.108
An exception that never expires makes a
gap permanent; an expired one is a ticket.

112
00:07:17.108 --> 00:07:21.947
A state string the kit doesn't know could
make a healthy device look unhealthy,

113
00:07:21.947 --> 00:07:26.257
or the reverse; only listed values
count, and unknown ones are flagged.

114
00:07:26.257 --> 00:07:29.971
A renamed column would make
every device read as empty;

115
00:07:29.971 --> 00:07:32.033
a missing column fails the run.

116
00:07:32.033 --> 00:07:34.673
And servers aren't proven
by the client report;

117
00:07:34.673 --> 00:07:38.850
the automation kit's Arc task finds
servers without the extension.

118
00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:43.950
Here's what done looks like.

119
00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:48.687
The inventory and the exception register
are current and named in the settings file.

120
00:07:48.687 --> 00:07:52.800
The state strings in the kit's
configuration match the test tenant.

121
00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:55.262
Every ticket has an owner within the week.

122
00:07:55.262 --> 00:07:59.113
The guide's summary query is filed
beside the per-device result.

123
00:07:59.113 --> 00:08:02.062
And the run folder is
recorded as evidence.

124
00:08:02.062 --> 00:08:06.840
Next, Chapter 3 reads the device state
files this chapter's collector writes,

125
00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:09.874
and checks prevention
against each ring's stage.

126
00:08:09.874 --> 00:08:11.040
Thanks for watching.
